{"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux"]]},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/ArchitectureGuide","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"sections":[],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Understand the architectural patterns that make Swidux work correctly with SwiftUI’s observation system."}],"seeAlsoSections":[{"anchor":"Explanation","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PluginArchitecture","doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/DesignPrinciples","doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SecurityPosture"],"title":"Explanation","generated":true}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"text":"Overview","anchor":"Overview","type":"heading"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Swidux relies on specific patterns to ensure correct ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" behavior, efficient rendering, and safe concurrency. This article explains the reasoning behind these patterns.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"The-Snapshot-Pattern","level":2,"type":"heading","text":"The Snapshot Pattern"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store.send()"},{"text":" copies the observer tree into a local struct, mutates the copy via the reducer, then assigns changed properties back. This happens internally via ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SwiduxObservable"},{"text":" — ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State(observer:)"},{"text":" packs, ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State.apply(_:to:)"},{"text":" unpacks.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The pattern exists for two reasons:","type":"text"}]},{"items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" equality checking.","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" The ","type":"text"},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" accessor checks ","type":"text"},{"code":"Equatable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and suppresses no-op notifications. Swift’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"_modify","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" accessor (used by ","type":"text"},{"code":"inout","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") fires notifications unconditionally. The snapshot pattern routes through ","type":"text"},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Cross-slice observation isolation."}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" The ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Swidux","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" macro generates a separate ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" class for each ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Slice","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" property. A view reading ","type":"text"},{"code":"store.items","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" won’t re-render when only ","type":"text"},{"code":"store.ui","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" changes, because they live on different observer objects.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"type":"orderedList"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Swidux"},{"type":"text","text":" macro generates the observer class tree and "},{"code":"SwiduxObservable","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" conformance automatically. Hand-written conformance is still possible for advanced cases — see "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SwiduxObservable","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Explicit equality guards ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"if x != state.x { x = state.x }"},{"type":"text","text":") are unnecessary. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"type":"text","text":" already checks equality on "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"set"},{"type":"text","text":". Unconditional assignment is safe."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"name":"Note","style":"note","type":"aside"},{"level":2,"text":"Dispatch Loop Detection","anchor":"Dispatch-Loop-Detection","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PersistencePlugin","type":"reference"},{"text":" warns if more than 100 changes are drained within a single debounce interval (the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"loopThreshold"},{"text":" parameter; 250 ms by default). It counts only dispatches that changed an ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/EntityStore","type":"reference"},{"text":", and it reports once per burst — the count starts over after a whole debounce interval with no drains. This usually means an effect or plugin dispatches an action on every state change, feeding the cycle it reacts to. A steady stream of edits, such as a slider drag, stays well under the threshold and is not reported.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"text":"Debounced Persistence","anchor":"Debounced-Persistence","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Each drain restarts the flush’s debounce timer, so a burst of edits is written once. The timer is never pushed further than ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"maxWait"},{"text":" past the oldest pending change (four debounce intervals, and at least a second, by default), so continuous edits still reach storage while they continue. A retry of a failed flush keeps its own schedule; a new edit postpones it only when the flush it schedules is due first and carries the failed batch anyway.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"text":"Reducer Weight","anchor":"Reducer-Weight","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Reducers run synchronously on the MainActor. Move O(n²) work into an ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/Effect","type":"reference"},{"text":" and dispatch the result back as an action.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"text":"Effect Threading","anchor":"Effect-Threading","type":"heading"},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Effects run on Swift concurrency’s cooperative thread pool via "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task { @concurrent in }"},{"type":"text","text":". Blocking calls ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Process.waitUntilExit()"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DispatchSemaphore.wait()"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Thread.sleep()"},{"type":"text","text":") hold threads hostage. If enough block, the pool starves and the MainActor freezes."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"name":"Important","style":"important","type":"aside"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Use async alternatives: ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"terminationHandler"},{"text":" + continuation instead of ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"waitUntilExit()"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task.sleep()"},{"type":"text","text":" instead of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Thread.sleep()"},{"text":", async file I\/O instead of ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Data(contentsOf:)"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"kind":"article","variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/swidux\/architectureguide"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"Swidux"}],"roleHeading":"Article","role":"article","title":"Architecture & Performance"},"references":{"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/Effect":{"role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","title":"Effect","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"Effect"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"Effect"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/Effect","abstract":[{"text":"Async work with cancellation metadata declared before the store starts it.","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/effect","type":"topic"},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/SecurityPosture":{"role":"collectionGroup","kind":"article","title":"Security Posture","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SecurityPosture","abstract":[{"text":"What Swidux’s soft controls actually guarantee, and what to do when you need a hard one.","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/securityposture","type":"topic"},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/SwiduxObservable":{"role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","title":"SwiduxObservable","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"protocol"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"SwiduxObservable"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"SwiduxObservable"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SwiduxObservable","abstract":[{"text":"Bridges a value-type state struct to an ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" class tree for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"per-property SwiftUI observation granularity.","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/swiduxobservable","type":"topic"},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/EntityStore":{"role":"symbol","title":"EntityStore","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"EntityStore"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"EntityStore"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/EntityStore","abstract":[{"text":"An ordered, keyed collection of identifiable entities that silently records","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"every mutation for downstream persistence.","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/entitystore","type":"topic"},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/PluginArchitecture":{"role":"article","kind":"article","title":"Plugin Architecture","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PluginArchitecture","abstract":[{"text":"Extend the dispatch cycle with lifecycle hooks — from invisible infrastructure to domain-specific features.","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/pluginarchitecture","type":"topic"},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux","kind":"symbol","title":"Swidux","abstract":[{"text":"Redux-style state management for SwiftUI, with macros for observability and ready-made plugins for persistence, undo, paywalls, version killswitches, and parental gates.","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/swidux","type":"topic","role":"collection"},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/DesignPrinciples":{"role":"article","title":"Design Principles","kind":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/DesignPrinciples","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The 10 rules that shape every Swidux app, and the reasoning behind them."}],"url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/designprinciples","type":"topic"},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/PersistencePlugin":{"type":"topic","kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/persistenceplugin","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PersistencePlugin","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Coalescing persistence plugin."}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"class","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"PersistencePlugin","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"PersistencePlugin","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"PersistencePlugin"}}}